  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nOn Thursday, the upper low will begin to eject into the central \nGreat Plains, with strong southwest flow and anomalously high column \nmoisture progged to persist over West TX. At the surface, a cold \nfront will be progressing southward over the TX Panhandle, and will \nmake steady southward progress through the day. Showers and storms \nare expected to continue ahead of the cold front mainly over the \nRolling Plains on Thursday with a continuing potential for heavy \nrain off the Caprock. Forecast shear and instability parameters \nsuggest a strong or marginally severe storm will again be possible \nThursday afternoon in the warm sector ahead of the front, but if \nfrontal progression is faster than currently anticipated, the threat \nfor both severe storms and heavy rain may shift to our south and \neast. Otherwise, our current expectation is for a general drying \ntrend to take hold beginning on Thursday and continue through Friday \nas the band of upper level moisture is displaced southward as flow \naloft weakens and becomes more zonal. \n\nThe other main story will be  relatively strong north winds and a \nfairly dramatic cooldown behind the front late Thursday through \nFriday. Surface pressure rises on the order of 4-6 mb in 3 hr may\nresult in a period of sustained winds near 30 mph Thursday \nafternoon through early Friday morning, and our coolest temperatures\nof the season so far are expected as a ~1035 mb surface high \nbuilds over most of the Great Plains states. Most locations will \nsee lows in the 40s on Thursday night, with temperatures likely \nto peak in the upper 50s at most on Friday afternoon. The surface \nridge will settle nearly directly overhead on Friday night, with \nthe combination of light winds and clearing skies setting the \nstage for lows in the mid 30s to low 40s on Saturday morning. \nLocations over the SW TX Panhandle and NW South Plains may even \nbriefly dip below freezing early Saturday morning, but confidence \non a bona fide first freeze is still low. After another cool day \non Saturday (highs in the 60s), temperatures will gradually warm \nback near normal Sunday into early next week. At this time, we \nexpect dry conditions across the region this weekend through at \nleast Monday before a potential return of shower chances by the \nmiddle of next week.",
